description.narrative:
1.1. Recommendations for amending the GEWE related legislation drafted;
1.2. Chapter on Gender equality under the National Human Rights Action Plan adopted;
1.3. Chapter/sub-chapter on LGBTQI and non-discrimination under the National Human Rights Action Plan adopted; 
1.4. Chapter/sub-chapter on elimination of harmful practices under the National Human Rights Action Plan adopted;
1.5. # of studies to establish evidence on gender inequality, social inclusion and gender-based violence for informed policy making conducted and disseminated;
1.6. # of oversight mechanisms utilized by the Parliament of Georgia to enhance GEWE;
1.7. Concepts of CARE Economy and Gender-responsive family policies developed at the national and local levels;
1.8. The National Concept for Women’s Economic Empowerment is adopted;

2.1. % of GEWE-related commitments under the National Human Rights Action Plan (incl. VAWG/DV NAP, elimination of harmful practices, WEE, LGBTIQ) implemented (UNSDCF 1.2.2);
2.2. % of changed attitudes among the state employees on GEWE and human rights issues;
2.3. Number of new GESI sensitive services in place;
2.4. % change in issued restrictive orders;
2.5. % of change in the state budget allocation for VAWG/DV services;
2.6. # of institutionalized training programs on GEWE;
2.7. # of public institutions with relevant internal policies and procedures to address GBV and harmful practices; 
2.8. % of  the topics from the International Technical Guidance on Sexuality Education integrated in the national curriculum on general education;

3.1. # of joint initiatives with the private sector to promote GEWE;
3.2. % of the population that does not consider gender equality as a zero-sum game (reject the statement that empowerment of women implies that men are deprived of their rights);
3.3. % of the population disagreeing with the statement that men have the final say in the family;
